{"name":"First Light","hex":"#d9e6ee","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"first light","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The time when light first appears in the morning; dawn, especially the start of dawn.","The first use of a telescope or other instrument to capture an astronomical image."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/first_light"}],"entries":[{"title":"First light (astronomy)","blocks":[{"text":"In astronomy, first light is the first use of a new instrument, typically a telescope, to take an astronomical image after it has been constructed. This is often not the first viewing using the telescope, as optical tests are usually performed beforehand to adjust the components."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Kepler_mission_first_light.tiff/lossy-page1-330px-Kepler_mission_first_light.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_light_(astronomy)","wikidata":"Q1306940"},{"title":"Chronology of the universe","blocks":[{"text":"The chronology of the universe describes the history and future of the universe according to the current understanding of physical cosmology. In this model, the earliest stage that is supported by observational evidence is known as inflation, which occurred 13.8 billion years ago. During this epoch, space underwent a period of extremely rapid expansion in a tiny fraction of a second. Once inflation ended, the energy that was responsible for it was converted into particles and radiation, heating up the universe into a very hot and dense state, initiating the Big Bang."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/WMAP_2012.png/330px-WMAP_2012.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chronology_of_the_universe","wikidata":"Q136407"},{"title":"First Light (2025 film)","blocks":[{"text":"First Light is a 2025 drama film written and directed by James J. Robinson, in his feature debut. A co-production of Philippines and Australia, it stars Ruby Ruiz, Kare Adea, Maricel Soriano, Emmanuel Santos, Rez Cortez, Soliman Cruz, and Kidlat Tahimik."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/ae/First_Light_2025_film_poster.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Light_(2025_film)","wikidata":"Q135987708"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Kepler_mission_first_light.tiff/lossy-page1-500px-Kepler_mission_first_light.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Kepler_mission_first_light.tiff/lossy-page1-1280px-Kepler_mission_first_light.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Kepler_mission_first_light.tiff/lossy-page1-500px-Kepler_mission_first_light.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKepler_mission_first_light.tiff"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Trifid_and_Lagoon_nebulae.jpg/500px-Trifid_and_Lagoon_nebulae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Trifid_and_Lagoon_nebulae.jpg/1280px-Trifid_and_Lagoon_nebulae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Trifid_and_Lagoon_nebulae.jpg/500px-Trifid_and_Lagoon_nebulae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"First released image from the Vera C. Rubin Observatory, the Trifid and Lagoon nebulae,[3] released on 23 June 2025.[4]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATrifid_and_Lagoon_nebulae.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Look-back_time_by_redshift.png/500px-Look-back_time_by_redshift.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Look-back_time_by_redshift.png/500px-Look-back_time_by_redshift.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The lookback time of extragalactic observations by their cosmological redshift up to z=20.[2]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALook-back_time_by_redshift.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/History-of-the-Universe.jpg/500px-History-of-the-Universe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/History-of-the-Universe.jpg/1280px-History-of-the-Universe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/History-of-the-Universe.jpg/500px-History-of-the-Universe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"  The NASA diagram shows the history of the universe from inflation until the present.[5]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHistory-of-the-Universe.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Hubble_ultra_deep_field.jpg/500px-Hubble_ultra_deep_field.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Hubble_ultra_deep_field.jpg/1280px-Hubble_ultra_deep_field.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Hubble_ultra_deep_field.jpg/500px-Hubble_ultra_deep_field.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Hubble Ultra Deep Fields often feature galaxies that are examples of what the early Stelliferous Era was like.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHubble_ultra_deep_field.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Hubble_-_infant_galaxy.jpg/500px-Hubble_-_infant_galaxy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Hubble_-_infant_galaxy.jpg/500px-Hubble_-_infant_galaxy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Another Hubble image shows an infant galaxy forming nearby, which means this happened very recently on the cosmological timescale. This shows that new galaxy formation in the universe is still occurring.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHubble_-_infant_galaxy.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Conditions_During_the_Era_of_Reionization_%28Illustration%29_%282023-122%29.jpg/500px-Conditions_During_the_Era_of_Reionization_%28Illustration%29_%282023-122%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Con